---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Badge } from '@/components/ui/badge';
import { getVenues } from '@/lib/db';

/* Counts and examples come from the database at build time: typing them in
   guarantees they go stale the next time a venue is added. */
const venues = getVenues();
const count = (tier: string) => venues.filter((v) => v.tier === tier).length;
const examples = (tier: string, n = 10) => venues.filter((v) => v.tier === tier).slice(0, n).map((v) => v.name).join(' · ');
---

<Layout title="About the stages" section="about" description="Why White Rabbit groups venues by a project's stage: workshop, full paper, journal.">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">A project's path, not a ranking</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[1.02rem] text-fg-2">
      This is not a ranking of venues. It is the path one project takes to finish: an idea gets aired at a
      <strong class="text-fg">workshop</strong>, the real result lands as a <strong class="text-fg">full paper with a poster</strong>,
      and the mature version is archived in a <strong class="text-fg">journal</strong>. Most work wants all three. The only
      place grades apply is the middle stage, where the same finished paper could go to two different calibres of venue.
    </p>

    <figure class="card my-8 p-4">
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 860 300" class="pyramid block h-auto w-full min-w-[620px]" role="img"
             aria-label="A project's three stages, left to right: Rabbit Hole, a workshop paper; Wonderland, a full paper with poster, which is either a Royal Flush or a Full House venue; and Looking Glass, the archival journal version.">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arw" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <text x="104" y="70" class="p-stage" text-anchor="middle">STAGE 1</text>
          <rect x="24" y="118" width="160" height="84" rx="12" fill="var(--rank-base)" fill-opacity=".16" stroke="var(--rank-base)" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <text x="104" y="150" class="p-label" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--rank-base)">Rabbit Hole</text>
          <text x="104" y="170" class="p-axis" text-anchor="middle">workshop paper</text>
          <text x="104" y="190" class="p-count" text-anchor="middle">{count('rabbit-hole')}</text>

          <line x1="192" y1="160" x2="246" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" opacity=".5" marker-end="url(#arw)" />
          <text x="219" y="150" class="p-axis" text-anchor="middle">grows into</text>

          <text x="404" y="70" class="p-stage" text-anchor="middle">STAGE 2</text>
          <rect x="256" y="82" width="296" height="180" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity=".25" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 5" />
          <text x="404" y="102" class="p-axis" text-anchor="middle">full paper + poster, pick your table</text>
          <rect x="274" y="114" width="260" height="60" rx="10" fill="var(--rank-top)" fill-opacity=".16" stroke="var(--rank-top)" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <text x="298" y="140" class="p-label" fill="var(--rank-top)">Royal Flush</text>
          <text x="516" y="140" class="p-count" text-anchor="end">{count('royal-flush')}</text>
          <text x="298" y="159" class="p-axis">the strongest venues</text>
          <rect x="274" y="186" width="260" height="60" rx="10" fill="var(--rank-mid)" fill-opacity=".16" stroke="var(--rank-mid)" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <text x="298" y="212" class="p-label" fill="var(--rank-mid)">Full House</text>
          <text x="516" y="212" class="p-count" text-anchor="end">{count('full-house')}</text>
          <text x="298" y="231" class="p-axis">strong and specialised</text>

          <line x1="560" y1="160" x2="614" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" opacity=".5" marker-end="url(#arw)" />
          <text x="587" y="150" class="p-axis" text-anchor="middle">extends into</text>

          <text x="716" y="70" class="p-stage" text-anchor="middle">STAGE 3</text>
          <rect x="624" y="118" width="184" height="84" rx="12" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity=".35" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <text x="716" y="150" class="p-label" text-anchor="middle">Looking Glass</text>
          <text x="716" y="170" class="p-axis" text-anchor="middle">journal · no deadline</text>
          <text x="716" y="190" class="p-count" text-anchor="middle">{count('looking-glass')}</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-[46ch] text-center text-xs text-muted">
        One project, three stages. Only the middle one is graded: a finished paper is the same paper whichever table you take
        it to, so that is the single place a venue's calibre changes the decision.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-fg-2">
      <section class="border-t border-border pt-5">
        <h2 class="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-bold text-fg">
          <span class="text-[0.66rem] font-bold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">Stage 1</span>
          <Badge variant="base">Rabbit Hole</Badge>
        </h2>
        <p>
          Where you jump in with a half-formed idea. Workshops: five pages, a position, and an argument you are not sure of
          yet. Low stakes by design, fast turnaround, and the room that tells you whether the idea survives contact with
          people. Skipping this stage is how a project discovers its flaw eighteen months late.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">{examples('rabbit-hole')}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-border pt-5">
        <h2 class="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-bold text-fg">
          <span class="text-[0.66rem] font-bold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">Stage 2</span>
          <Badge variant="top">Royal Flush</Badge>
          <Badge variant="mid">Full House</Badge>
        </h2>
        <p>
          The full paper, and the poster that goes with it: the result the project is actually for. This is the only stage
          with grades, because it is the only one where the same finished paper has a choice of table:
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-fg">Royal Flush</strong>: the best hand in the deck and the rarest. Single-digit-to-twenty percent acceptance, recognised by acronym alone.</p>
        <p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-fg">Full House</strong>: a strong hand that wins most tables. Specialised venues with real review and a more focused audience, often where the more interesting arguments happen.</p>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">Royal Flush: {examples('royal-flush')}</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">Full House: {examples('full-house')}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-border pt-5">
        <h2 class="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-bold text-fg">
          <span class="text-[0.66rem] font-bold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase">Stage 3</span>
          <Badge variant="off">Looking Glass</Badge>
        </h2>
        <p>
          The reflected version: longer, fuller, archival. Journals have no deadline, no cycle and no countdown. You submit
          when the work is ready and the clock never applies, which is why White Rabbit shows them as <em>rolling submission</em>
          and never counts them down. It is the last stage precisely because it is the one the calendar cannot rush.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">{examples('looking-glass')}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl bg-surface-2 p-5">
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-bold text-fg">What this is not</h2>
        <p>
          A ranking of research quality, and not a rule. Plenty of good work skips a stage, and plenty of the best ideas in
          systems appeared first as a Rabbit Hole workshop paper and never went further. Read it as the route most projects
          benefit from, not a ladder you owe anyone.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Stage 2's two grades track how a venue <em>behaves</em> (selectivity, breadth of audience), not how good any
          particular paper in it is. It is one judgement call recorded in one field of the database; if a venue looks
          misplaced, that is a one-line change and a reasonable pull request.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .pyramid { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .pyramid :global(.p-label) { font: 700 14px Inter, sans-serif; }
  .pyramid :global(.p-count) { font: 700 13px 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; fill: var(--text-secondary); }
  .pyramid :global(.p-axis) { font: 500 11px Inter, sans-serif; fill: var(--text-muted); }
  .pyramid :global(.p-stage) { font: 700 10px Inter, sans-serif; fill: var(--text-muted); letter-spacing: 0.12em; }
</style>
